  <article class="investigation-guide">
    <section class="vg-hero"><h1>Follow an observation to its owner</h1><p>Use the source time, resource and owner before deciding what needs repair. Commands below run on the operator's host.</p></section>
    <section id="evidence" class="vg-glass"><h2>Published evidence · Ansible</h2><p>Inspect the observation, cluster and run IDs in Evidence details. Reloading does not publish new evidence. A stale or invalid snapshot cannot establish current health.</p><p>Use <code>make doctor</code> to inspect the current target, then <code>make publish-evidence</code> to retry publication for a provisioned run. Inspect the latest failed attempt separately from the last success. Full runbooks: <code>docs/operations.md</code> and <code>docs/must-progress.md</code>.</p></section>
    <section id="vault" class="vg-glass"><h2>Vault health · Vault / Ansible</h2><p>The live seal-chain checks observe Seal Vault, every main Vault member and the seal Agent deployment every 15 seconds while this tab is visible. Results expire after 30 seconds. Agent readiness does not prove a Transit decrypt or future recovery. An unavailable endpoint can mean a network, trust, sealed-state or service failure.</p><p>Inspect individual seal states with <code>make doctor</code>, then use <code>make verify</code> for full health and convergence. Follow <code>docs/troubleshooting.md</code> and <code>docs/credential-operations.md</code>. Do not replace a CA or reset data to repair a leaf or reachability failure.</p></section>
    <section id="identity" class="vg-glass"><h2>Identity discovery · Ansible</h2><p>Discovery proves the identity endpoint answered with the expected document shape. It does not prove a user can authenticate or has the intended policies.</p><p>Check Keycloak readiness, ingress trust and host/node clock agreement, then use <code>make identity-verify</code> to test personas. Follow <code>docs/session-contract.md</code> and <code>docs/troubleshooting.md</code>. Keep certificate and logout-signature verification enabled.</p></section>
    <section id="delivery" class="vg-glass"><h2>Secret delivery · VSO / Terraform / Ansible</h2><p>Terraform owns the declared resources; Ansible configures Vault and seed values; VSO delivers them. Runtime status reads the named VSO resources and deployment readiness without reading Kubernetes Secrets.</p><p>A rotation receipt confirms a Vault write or reports uncertainty. A later sync timestamp or ready deployment alone does not correlate that receipt with the delivered value. Use <code>make vso-status</code> and the scoped recovery guidance in <code>docs/operations.md</code>. Do not repeat an uncertain write automatically.</p></section>
    <section id="components" class="vg-glass"><h2>PKI and Transit · Terraform / Ansible</h2><p>Terraform owns dedicated proof fixtures. On the host, <code>make component-proof</code> tests certificate name/policy restrictions, client-chain validation, proof-of-possession revocation and expiry, then encrypt/decrypt, key rotation and rewrap while preserving old ciphertext access.</p><p>Use <code>make publish-evidence</code> to publish the sanitized proof summary after a successful provisioned run. Compare its input digest and time with the latest automation attempt. <code>make destruction-guard-proof</code> verifies a destroy plan is refused; it never applies it. See <code>docs/vault-component-proofs.md</code>. Keep generated keys, plaintext, snapshots and raw responses out of browser evidence.</p></section>
    <section id="audit" class="vg-glass"><h2>Audit gaps · Vault / collector</h2><p>The live feed is a volatile metadata buffer. A separate bounded PVC archive retains a narrower metadata projection. Restart changes its instance ID and clears earlier entries. Filters and pagination cover only the recent buffer, not a durable archive.</p><p>Check collector reachability and Vault connections. The independent stdout audit device is a separate path; its current health is not established by this page. Follow <code>collector/README.md</code> and <code>docs/security-model.md</code>. Never paste tokens or raw sensitive responses into diagnostics.</p></section>
    <section id="ownership" class="vg-glass">
      <h2>Policy and ownership · Terraform / Ansible / VSO</h2>
      <p>The <NuxtLink to="/explorer">policy and ownership explorer</NuxtLink> shows published declarations from <code>policies/ownership-map.json</code> and <code>policies/boundary-matrix.json</code>. Compare source hashes, input digest and observation identity before interpreting them.</p>
      <p>Expected operations do not establish actual access. Run <code>make boundary</code> on the operator's host for the four maintenance identities, or <code>make observer-boundary</code> for the named Kubernetes observer checks. UI roles do not grant these capabilities.</p>
      <p>For a field dispute, inspect its declared owner and exact runtime exception before reconciling with the owning phase. Follow <code>docs/ownership.md</code>. Keep credentials and raw policy payloads out of browser diagnostics.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="observability" class="vg-glass"><h2>Metrics and retained metadata · Terraform / collector</h2><p>The <NuxtLink to="/observability">observability view</NuxtLink> reads a fixed endpoint for the optional OpenTelemetry or Prometheus integration and the independently optional bounded metadata archive through the authenticated BFF. It never accepts a destination, query or file path from the browser.</p><p>Both integrations default to disabled; the core lab and live audit ring require neither backend nor archive. The live ring and counters reset on collector restart. Committed archive rows retain their original IDs and times. Retention is at most seven days or 64 MiB, with pruning on writes and a 30-second maintenance interval. Storage failures, queue drops and corruption indicate gaps, not complete delivery.</p><p>Keep the independent stdout device enabled. Follow docs/observability.md for access checks, retention proof and recovery. Local PVCs do not prove recovery after host loss or provide external audit custody.</p></section>
    <section id="consumer" class="vg-glass"><h2>Agent database consumer · Terraform / Ansible / Vault Agent</h2><p>The sg-agent-db application reads one canary table using a mode-600 password file on a memory volume. Only its Agent sidecar mounts the audience-bound Kubernetes JWT. No Vault token or Kubernetes Secret is delivered to the application.</p><p>VSO delivers the existing application credentials through a Kubernetes Secret; this Agent pattern avoids that object but adds a sidecar and requires the application to reopen the file. Its readiness observes actual SQL access. The Agent card is a published readiness snapshot, not a live lease or rotation receipt.</p><p>Run <code>make consumer-proof</code> on the host to verify database scope, identity denial, credential replacement and pod recovery. Follow <code>docs/agent-database-consumer.md</code>. Memory delivery does not prove database restoration or recover a failed Vault cluster.</p></section>
